WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.850
Film Courage: If you had someone sitting in
this room out here (this beautiful lobby),

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:07.649
who’d never written a screenplay and you
were going to give them a five minute screenplay

00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:12.360
lesson (in five minutes) how would you start
it, what would it be?

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.190
Gordy Hoffman, Screenwriter, Founder and Judge
of the BlueCat Screenplay Competition: Well,

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:22.220
I would just ask them if they had an idea
and then I would just say pull out your laptop

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:27.290
or your notebook with your pen and start writing
it.

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:33.650
I wouldn’t even tell them about format,
I would just be like describe the movie.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.200
Everybody knows what a movie is.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:44.590
Everybody has seen it, like our visual vocabulary
is so high especially now 2018/2019.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.940
I mean anybody can describe it.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.470
The problem is when we start educating people
we’re like “Read these 6 books, take this

00:00:54.470 --> 00:01:01.630
course, do this stuff, watch this video.”

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:06.750
But that’s what I would do if someone is
like I want to write a movie and I don’t

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.760
know anything about it I’d be like “What
do you want to write about?

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.510
What’s your idea?”

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:13.870
And they probably have the idea.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:15.880
They’re not like “Well, I don’t know
what the idea is.”

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.761
They’re either going to be like “Well,
I want to write about this guy who wants to

00:01:19.761 --> 00:01:20.761
do this thing.”

00:01:20.761 --> 00:01:25.600
And I’ll be like “Okay, start describing
it, just describing it.”

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.140
And then I might say something like “Don’t
describe their thoughts and don’t describe

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.220
their feelings.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.290
Don’t describe the insides.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:38.170
Just describe what you would see on a movie
screen.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:39.550
Just like EXT.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.540
HOUSE, DAY.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.600
Man walks across and gets in the car, drives
away.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.810
Do not care about doing anything wrong.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:54.659
Just spill your guts, just tell us the movie,
write down the whole movie.

00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:56.650
We’ll figure out all that other stuff later.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:02.590
We can figure out where things are supposed
to be later, you can figure out everything.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:06.810
I mean there are so many stories about that
where they didn’t know what they were doing.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:11.470
You know they wrote BRIDESMAIDS that way,
they didn’t know…they bought Screenwriting

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.800
For Dummies or something like that, right?

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.570
So it’s like that fresh voice.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:17.570
Uh!

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:22.400
It breaks my heart thinking about all the
people who think they have, because we’ve

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:29.000
created this industry where it’s all coded
and they have to learn, you have to learn

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.500
structure, you have to learn this, you have
to learn all these things.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.370
You’ve got to know what a screenplay is,
man.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:36.209
You have to know what the rules are before
you can break them.

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:44.040
That’s all crap to sell books and all sorts
of stuff it doesn’t…we…gosh…please…bring

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.540
them in that don’t know anything about movies
and maybe we’ll have better movies.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.340
I mean that’s the problem it’s like all
this other stuff.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:56.930
Bring in that person that’s just like…I
have as a teacher, I’ve had people come

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:02.170
in with formatting that’s just like…they
don’t know what…they don’t have access

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:05.760
to Final Draft, I don’t know what they’re
writing?

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:14.120
And it’s the most authentic, slice of life,
funny, succinct, observational genius stuff

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:19.110
and the reason why it exists is because they
haven’t learned anything about screenwriting

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:20.930
yet.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:26.550
And then once you start teaching them, then
all of the sudden they don’t know what they’re

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.550
doing.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:32.370
Then they lose their emotional connection,
they lose that story…four-year-olds know

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:35.850
how to tell a story, they know how to tell
a story.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.460
Ask a five-year-old “What happened today
at school?”

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.110
“Well, we went in…”

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:44.430
Beginning, middle and end, the inciting incident,
you can tape and go Inciting incident…see

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:48.860
that’s what you guys at Film Courage should
do, you should interview a friggin’ ten-year-old

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:52.530
and then put the little flags in and be like
“Wow!

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:59.090
They know all the structure already so I don’t
have to go to so-and-so person’s course.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:03.760
I don’t have to take Gordy’s class”
or anything.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.569
You don’t have to spend any money and keep
your voice.

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:09.550
That’s the most important thing.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:15.170
It’s like don’t lose your voice, don’t
lose your instincts, your childlike instincts

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:16.870
for telling stories.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:21.400
That’s what guides us and I always go back
to that like “I don’t care!”

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:27.389
The thing I just wrote for somebody that in
all likelihood people who are watching this

00:04:27.389 --> 00:04:32.150
video will see, I don’t know what the structure
is, I don’t know where the act breaks are

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:37.279
in it and it’s over 150 pages, okay?

00:04:37.279 --> 00:04:44.050
And it’s probably going to be seen by people
watching this video, directed by a very, very

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.080
good top director.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.569
This is going to happen and I have no idea
what the structure of that is.

00:04:50.569 --> 00:04:56.740
I literally just took my ideas, plotted out
the thing, now am I following the rules of

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:02.180
character and emotional engagement to develop,
thing you can reflect back on and by like

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.580
“How do I make this better?”

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.249
But when you’re telling me, when somebody
is like “What’s the five minute version

00:05:08.249 --> 00:05:12.520
of somebody who wants to write a movie, watch
this video, what I just said.

